# The Maiden Who Became a Tiger!
Tamil title: புலியான பூவை!

When I finished reading, that woman appeared before my mind’s eye, and I exclaimed repeatedly in wonder and praise:

“A warrior’s salute to you, jewel among women! Honour and virtue have not utterly perished from the world only because a few noble women like you occasionally act with courage. Radiant lamp who revealed to the earth the unique glory of womankind, I bow at your feet!

“People speak as though women possess only dark eyes that enchant, lips like kovvai fruit that delight, and sacred hands that lull a child to sleep. They say women are so delicate that merely hearing a harsh word causes their hearts to suffer and their faces to lose their glow; they speak of maidens only as being like the fragile anicham flower.

“But, lady, the fearless heart you displayed while others stood stunned may be called an ornament to the whole earth. Long live your honoured name! May the renown and dignity of women flourish!”

Yes, my younger brother, that jewel of womankind performed a heroic deed worthy of everyone’s praise.

Seven hundred police officers stood ready with batons and guns, yet struggled helplessly, not knowing what to do. From inside a locked building came screams: “Alas! Danger! Danger! God, save us! Come running, come!”

The police could fire and force their way in. But what if the bullets struck not the perpetrators but those trapped in danger? That fear rendered them inactive.

The building was a school. Children from six to ten years old were trapped inside. Two frenzied men shouted that they would torture the boys and girls, hack them into pieces, and throw them away. Somehow the two had entered the school, bound the pupils’ hands and feet, and imprisoned the teachers. They carried knives and guns. How those children must have trembled!

For six hours, ninety-two children and three teachers remained trapped. The two men inside roared: “Bring us what we ask for respectfully and take your precious children away. If you enter to attack us, we shall heap up the slaughtered children before you capture us!”

Do you know what those cruel men demanded, my brother? They wanted 1,824,000 rupees.

Picture that scene once in your mind. Is it not truly shocking?

It was at such a moment that a heroic feeling surged from somewhere within that noble woman. The young scions of families, living portraits, were bound and weeping: little boys and girls of an age to be fondled and played with; one a tiny bird delighting all with dimples, another a deer bringing joy with mischief in its eyes, another a cuckoo giving pleasure through song, another a peacock dispelling anxiety through its happy dance. They were children whose baby speech sweetened the heart, of the age at which a mother kisses the crown of the head and sends them to school.

Two criminals threatened them. There were knives in their hands and derangement in their minds. They might do anything and fear nothing, unable to distinguish crime from good or wrong from right. At any moment they might shoot the children down.

The more she looked upon them, the more tears burst forth. But what use were tears? Something had to be done to remove the danger, and done quickly.

The maiden became a tiger. She sprang upon one of the men. She possessed no weapon but fearlessness, but what weapon can confer greater strength? She seized the knife in the madman’s hand and struck him on the head with it. His deranged head split and he fell. That was the first stage of victory: the building’s door could now be opened.

The other madman moved to kill the woman, but the police who entered shot him down. The children survived and the townspeople rejoiced. One of the two men died in hospital; the other was thrown into a cage.

Many must have stood with streaming eyes, pouring out love at the noble woman’s feet and saying, “Divine one! You are the visible god of our family!” Even we feel moved to bless her name.

My brother, the woman’s name was Karttori. One of the two men was Arturo and the other Osvaldo. Let their names be shaped by their country and language. We may delight in giving the woman a name worthy of her courage: daughter of a warrior clan!

Do not imagine that an enthusiastic author invented this to display the dignity of womankind, or that it is fiction showing a talent for heroic narrative. It is a true event that occurred some years ago in the Italian town rendered in the source as Perajana.

I thought of the two madmen brandishing blades before those innocent children and trembled. I wondered why humanity had died within them. Then I remembered that they were mad: how could they know what they were doing with disordered minds? That thought gave me some peace.

From there, my brother, my mind leapt to the politics of our country.

Just as those two Italian madmen bound schoolchildren, threatened them with knives and death, and tried to extort money, Congressmen keep the people of this country without clarity, display the two terrifying instruments of repression and financial power, threaten destruction, and work according to a plan to steal votes.

When others stood stunned and inactive, one noble woman fought without fear, prevented the murderers’ crime, and won. In the same way, we stand against Congress in this election. Every one of us needs the fearless heart she possessed.

She did not panic that the villains had a knife and gun or were attacking recklessly. She seized and cast away the cruel man’s blade. We need the courage to act likewise. Those seated in authority not only possess abundant weapons; many of them have developed a near-frenzied anger toward us.

They think: “We were able to bind with a cord of affection even Periyar, who made all these people into capable persons. And now these mere youngsters have set out to fight us!” The very thought fills them with rage. We understand that well.

*Arappor*, 17 August 1962.

---NOTES---
The Italian personal and place names are transliterated conservatively from Tamil because the scans do not supply Roman spellings. The source’s move from the rescue incident to an election analogy is preserved as a distinct transition. `பூவை` carries both maiden and flower associations; “maiden” is chosen in the title because the article repeatedly contrasts conventional feminine delicacy with tiger-like courage.
